      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed intervention is to add approximately 1–2 at.% Zr to W38Ta36Cr15V11 during fabrication.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding approximately 1–2 at.% Zr will suppress Cr-V-rich precipitate formation at 8 dpa.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Zr atoms will segregate primarily to grain boundaries and interfaces.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Grain-boundary Zr segregation will reduce local chemical gradients involving Cr.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Grain-boundary Zr segregation will reduce the diffusive mobility of Cr.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Reduced Cr mobility will inhibit the nucleation and growth of Cr-V-rich precipitates.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Zr addition will not adversely affect the alloy's beneficial nanocrystalline structure.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The nanocrystalline structure is responsible for the baseline alloy's low irradiation hardening and radiation resistance.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Zr-doped alloy will preserve the low hardening of unmodified W38Ta36Cr15V11.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Zr-doped alloy will preserve the radiation resistance of unmodified W38Ta36Cr15V11.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding 1–2 at.% Zr will retain practical bulk manufacturability.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "At 8 dpa, the Zr-doped alloy will have a significantly lower Cr-V-rich precipitate density or volume fraction than the unmodified alloy.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "After irradiation, the Zr-doped alloy's hardness will remain comparable to that of the unmodified baseline.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
